  "course_description": "Highly focused courses, drawing on multiple disciplines of the humanities and social sciences; may be repeated for credit when topics vary.",
  "course_notes": "This class begins with a simple premise; the way we take care of ourselves is political. The modern wellness industry, valued at 4.4 trillion dollars in 2024, is replete with products, practices, and potions that are advertised as solutions to our unwellness. But, who benefits from the consumerist framing of wellness, and who gets left behind? This class pays close attention to wellness practices that are rooted in centuries-old religious traditions and communities. What happens when practices are extracted from their communal contexts, disconnected from ethical worldviews, and repackaged as tools for individual self-improvement and increased productivity? Is productivity and efficiency under capitalism the only, or even the primary, vision of wellbeing available to us? How might deep reflection upon the religious traditions behind modern self-care practices help support us in envisioning a deeper understanding of human flourishing and an ethical, relational, and collective model for wellbeing? Students in this class will explore the tension between individualist and collectivist models of care. Together, we will examine the history of self-care as a concept, contemporary scholarship on religion and wellness, and writings that connect practices of care to liberation from systems of oppression. Throughout the semester, students will have opportunities to practice self-care and community-care through in-class activities and assignments.",
